  "strategy": "Appach and rationale:\nWe propose a multi-resolution evolution strategy to improve the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap constant. The core idea is to combine binary optimization with continuous relaxation and multi-resolution scaling to escape local optima. This approach addresses the limitations of the provided algorithm by introducing:\n1. Resolution scaling - optimizing solutions at multiple grid resolutions (n=200,400,600,800) and combining the best results\n2. Fuzzy representation - maintaining both crisp binary solutions and fuzzy (continuous) variants for better exploration\n3. Hierarchical optimization - using coarse grids to inform finer grids, with different search strategies at each level\n4. Statistical initialization - generating candidates using low-discrepancy sequences for broader exploration\n\nThe motivation is that:\n- Binary search methods might get stuck in local optima\n- Continuous relaxation allows better exploration\n- Coarser resolutions help escaping local optima in finer grids\n- Statistical initialization reduces bias from fixed parent\n\nMathematical specification:\n1. Representation:\n   - Primary solution: Binary vector h of size n (initially 600) with exactly n/2 ones\n   - Secondary surrogate: Continuous interpolation h_cont in [0,1] of length n\n   - Fitness metric: C_5 = max_k integrate h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx * (2/n) [using provided correlation method]\n\n2. Formulation:\n   Minimize C_5 subject to:\n     - h[i] in {0,1} (binary) \n     - sum h[i] = n/2\n   For continuous interpolation, the constraints become:\n     - 0 <= h_cont[i] <= 1\n     - sum h_cont[i] \u2248 n/2\n\n3. Optimization:\n   We use two formulations:\n   a) Binary version (exact): Min C_5 with h in {0,1} and sum h = n/2\n   b) Continuous version (relaxed): Min C_5 with h in [0,1] and sum h = n/2\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Initialization:\n   a) Start with given initial_h_values (n=600)\n   b) Generate additional seeds using Sobol low-discrepancy sequence\n   c) Create fuzzy (continuous) version by interpolation\n\n2. Multi-resolution phase:\n   For grid sizes in [200,400,600,800]:\n     a) Project parent solution to coarser grid\n     b) Run genetic algorithm with:\n        - Population size=min(100, sqrt(n))\n        - Crossover probability=0.9\n        - Mutation probability=0.1\n        - Selection: deterministic crowding\n     c) Collect best solution and error\n\n3. Evolution strategy:\n   MAin phase (n=600):\n     a) Synchronous update rule:\n        h_bin[i] = clip( min(1, max(0, h_cont[i])) \n     b) Fuzzy candidate innovation if the binary version of h_cont is worse than best solution:\n        h_cont_new = h_cont + \u03b5 (where \u03b5 is random normal noise)\n     c) Repair via temperature-based swap (swap 0 and 1 if improves binary objective)\n\n   Pseudo-code:\n   function project_to(n_target, h_source)\n        scale = n_target / len(h_source)\n        h_resampled = np.interp(np.linspace(0,1,n_target), np.linspace(0,1,len(h_source)), h_source)\n        return ternary_project(h_resampled, 0.5)  [force to closest binary with sum(n/2)]\n\n   4. Local search:\n     Try up to 30 random swaps (0\u21941) and accept if improves C_5, with probability exp(-\u0394C/temperature)\n\n5. Validation:\n   - After each candidate, enforce validity: 0<=h[i]<=1 and sum h==n/2\n   - Periodically clamp values to [0,1] and project\n   - Maintain a memoization table: (n, tuple(h)) -> C_5 value to avoid duplicates\n\n6. Meta-heuristics:\n   - Hybrid algorithm combining:\n        SA for exploration (50% of runs)\n        GAs (deterministic crowding) for exploitation (50%)\n        Local search after each generation\n\nSearch choices:\n1. population_size: 50 (small for speed)\n2. Mutation: bit-flips (swap 0/1) with probability 0.05\n3. Crossover: uniform crossover with probability 0.9\n4. Temperature schedule: exp(-5*sqrt(time_left))\n5. Resolution list: [200,400,600,800] to cover increasing scales\n\nComputational budget:\n- Each resolution level: time = max(10, 1300/4) seconds\n- With 4 resolutions: 3200 seconds if not optimized\n- Self-adaptive timing per_resolution:\n\n  Algorithm for estimating time_left:\n   - Use system time if available (but without file system)\n   - If at start: time_start = time.time()\n\n  For each generation:\n   - Elapsed = min(time.time()-time_start, budget_s)\n   - time_left = budget_s - int(elapsed)\n\n  Adaptive cooling/sampling based on time_left.\n\nRobustness:\n- Use fallback solution if:\n    - No improvement for 30 generations\n    - Time <= 5% budget_s left\n  Fallback: Uses the best solution from lower resolutions\n- For invalid candidates (sum not n/2): Project using ternary closest majority\n- For numerical instability: Ignore candidate if correlation computation throws exception\n\nValidation and return contract:\n1. After candidates:\n   - Re-evaluate C_5 using provided compute_c5()\n   - Ensure sum(np.round(h)) is exactly n/2\n   - If not valid, apply project_binary() anyway\n2. Final selection: Best candidate across all resolutions\n3. Output checks:\n   - 0 <= all(h) <= 1\n   - abs(sum(h)-n/2)/n < 1e-5\n   - C_5 was computed by the same method as parent\n\nThe multi-resolution approach allows leveraging coarser grids' simplicity to guide finer optimizations, with fuzzy representations mediating between discrete and continuous spaces for better overall exploration.",
